{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310625,"nonce":"q/B/wXdkIuZHgbFY0NhIjs+M0sI=","title":"GGUFLoader returning null / failing during the per-tensor decode loop for files \u003e2 GiB — root cause was i32 truncation of u64 tensor ...","message":"**Status: FIXED for the offset-truncation symptom.** All 291 tensors of\nLlama-3-8B Q4_K_M decode successfully (top half offsets reach `offHi=1`,\nabsolute positions past 4 GiB). The trailing SIGILL on llama-chat is a\n*different* problem — OOM-territory on 16 GB RAM since the 8B model\ndequant'd to F32 wants ~32 GB resident (the user's plan acknowledged\nthis; phase 4b's `Linear`-on-`QTensor` is the path forward).\n\n\n`Bytes.sub(pos:Int, len:Int)` and `Bytes.getInt32(pos:Int):Int` take i32\npositions. `GGUFReader.readU64Truncated()` was reading just the low 32\nbits of a u64 GGUF offset. For tensors past the 2 GiB mark of the file:\n\n```\n[Q4KM-PROBE] name=blk.15.ffn_gate.weight offset=-2134851584\n              raw.length=0 rows=4096 cols=14336\n```\n\nThe sign-extended negative offset failed the `pos \u003c 0` check inside\n`haxe_bytes_sub_i64`, which returned an empty bytes view; the QTensor\nconstructor then rejected the too-short slice and returned null.\n\n\nThree coordinated changes:\n\n1. `runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs` — added `haxe_bytes_sub_u64lh(bytes,\n   pos_lo, pos_hi, len)` and `haxe_bytes_sub_base_u64lh(bytes, base,\n   off_lo, off_hi, len)`. The base-aware variant performs the\n   add-with-carry in u64 so callers never have to express it in Haxe.\n2. `compiler/haxe-std/rayzor/Bytes.hx` + `runtime_mapping.rs` — exposed\n   the new helpers as `Bytes.subU64(...)` and\n   `Bytes.subWithBase(base, offLo, offHi, len)`. (Both registered\n   under `rayzor_Bytes`, `haxe_io_Bytes`, and `Bytes` so any typing\n   path picks them up.)\n3. `nue/nue/loader/GGUFReader.hx` — split `TensorInfo.offset:Int` into\n   `offsetLo:Int` + `offsetHi:Int`, read both halves via\n   `bytes.getInt32(pos)` and `bytes.getInt32(pos+4)`, and switched\n   `tensorBytes(info)` to call\n   `bytes.subWithBase(dataStart, info.offsetLo, info.offsetHi, size)`.\n\n\nAfter the offset fix, llama-chat reaches `ArchRegistry.build(meta,\nweights)` (all 291 tensors loaded) and then traps with\n`udf #0xc11f` somewhere in JIT'd code. The 16 GB box is on 14 GB of\nswap; with Llama-3-8B Q4_K_M dequantising to ~22 GB of F32 tensors,\nthe trap is almost certainly memory-exhaustion-adjacent (malloc\nfailure, paged-out JIT page, etc.), not a separate logic bug. Phase\n4b (`Linear` dispatching directly on `QTensor` — see\n[[project_nue_ml_framework]]) is the architectural fix; the proper\n1B model would also avoid it.\n\n\n- [[bugs_anon_field_scramble]] — same shape of bug for cross-file\n  anon-struct reads; previously the `TensorInfo` field-access garbage\n  that was blamed on the decode crash. The `typedef → class` change\n  on `TensorInfo` (kept) made cross-file `Array\u003cTensorInfo\u003e` reads\n  return correct values.\n- [[bugs_static_inline_var_cross_context]] — the *preceding* bug on\n  this code path; fixing it surfaced this one.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_gguf_tensor_decode_crash` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}